PR #1922  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Missing subword LR/SC instructions force software to round up to word/doubleword reservations, which traps on CHERI bounds-checked systems where capabilities are narrower than the rounded region.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:50-51  _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED
      This macro is the exact 'granule rounding assumption' the security spec describes as the violation mechanism. It rounds a sub-word pointer DOWN to the nearest 4-byte boundary: `(uint32_t *)((__uintptr_t)(p) - ((__uintptr_t)(p) % 4))`. On CHERI, if the original pointer is capability-bounded to exactly 1 or 2 bytes, the rounded-down address falls outside the capability's authorized range, causing a bounds fault on the subsequent lr.w. This is the root cause of every subword atomic fallback violation.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:74-96  _atomic_cmpset_masked_word
      Loops calling atomic_fcmpset_32() on the already-rounded-down word address (addr was passed through _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED by all callers). atomic_fcmpset_32 issues lr.w on a word-aligned address that may fall outside the original capability bounds. The loop retries only when the masked bits still match, so it can spin indefinitely if the other halfword keeps changing — a secondary fairness concern, but the primary CHERI issue is the lr.w bounds violation.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:100-114  _atomic_fcmpset_masked_word
      Single-attempt masked fcmpset on the rounded-down word address. Reads *addr (full word at rounded-down location) to merge in the caller's *old value, then calls atomic_fcmpset_32 which issues lr.w. On CHERI, *addr already reads memory outside the subword capability bounds, and the subsequent lr.w inside atomic_fcmpset_32 traps. The single-attempt design is intentional (per atomic(9) spurious-failure contract), but the capability violation is the real issue.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:118-127  atomic_cmpset_8
      Byte cmpset: computes shift from _ATOMIC_BYTE_SHIFT, then passes the _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED(addr) result to _atomic_cmpset_masked_word. The uint8_t* is cast to uint32_t* at a rounded-down address. lr.w reservation covers 4 bytes when the caller's CHERI capability only authorizes 1 byte. Under Zabhlrsc, this would use native lr.b/sc.b instructions operating on the exact byte address with no granule rounding.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:131-144  atomic_fcmpset_8
      Byte fcmpset: same pattern as cmpset_8 — rounds uint8_t* down to uint32_t* via _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED, then calls _atomic_fcmpset_masked_word which internally calls atomic_fcmpset_32 → lr.w. On failure it extracts the original byte from the word value. The lr.w inside atomic_fcmpset_32 traps on CHERI when the capability for the byte pointer doesn't cover the full word.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:148-157  atomic_cmpset_16
      Halfword cmpset: same delegation chain through _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED + _atomic_cmpset_masked_word. The uint16_t* is rounded down to uint32_t*. lr.w covers a full 4-byte word when the caller only holds a 2-byte capability. Under Zabhlrsc this would use native lr.h/sc.h. The 0xffff mask covers a halfword but the reservation granule is the full word.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:161-174  atomic_fcmpset_16
      Halfword fcmpset via rounded-down word fcmpset_32. This is the core 'granule rounding' pattern: the caller holds a uint16_t* capability but the implementation widens the access granule to 32 bits. The lr.w inside atomic_fcmpset_32 accesses memory beyond the 2-byte capability bounds, causing a CHERI bounds fault. All callers of this function on CHERI+RISC-V are affected.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:178-187  atomic_load_acq_8
      Acquire byte load implemented as atomic_load_acq_32 on the enclosing word via _ATOMIC_WORD_ALIGNED, then shift+mask to extract the byte. atomic_load_acq_32 (from machine/atomic.h) does a plain load + fence, not lr.w — so this specific code path is NOT directly affected by the lr.w bounds violation. However, it still reads 4 bytes when the caller's capability only authorizes 1 byte, which is a capability bounds violation on CHERI. The plain load on the rounded-down address traps just like lr.w would.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:191-201  atomic_load_acq_16
      Acquire halfword load via atomic_load_acq_32 on enclosing word. Same analysis as atomic_load_acq_8: the 32-bit load touches memory outside the uint16_t capability bounds. Even though this uses a plain load (not lr.w), CHERI capability bounds checking applies to ALL memory accesses, so the load on the rounded-down address traps.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:208-219  atomic_set_16
      Halfword atomic set loops: loads via atomic_load_16 (which on RISC-V resolves to atomic_load_acq_16 from _atomic_subword.h, i.e. word-rounded load), then loops calling atomic_fcmpset_16 which internally rounds down to word and issues lr.w. Both the load and the lr.w access memory outside the uint16_t capability bounds. The retry loop amplifies exposure: each iteration re-issues lr.w on the violating address.
  [BUG] sys/sys/_atomic_subword.h:222-234  atomic_clear_16
      Halfword atomic clear loops via the same path as atomic_set_16: atomic_load_16 (word-rounded load) + atomic_fcmpset_16 (word-rounded lr.w/sc.w loop). Same capability bounds violation. Structurally identical to atomic_set_16 — the only difference is the bitwise operation (v & ~bit vs v | bit). Every iteration issues lr.w on a potentially unauthorized word-aligned address.
